course: dictionary
lesson: 5717
title: Lookout: a watcher, a view, a warning
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: A lookout is a person or animal who keeps watch, so they can warn the others if danger comes. «Meerkats stand up tall on their back legs as lookouts for hawks and jackals.» The meerkat on guard is the lookout, and the words around it show its job.
explain: Lookout can also be a place. «Serengeti lions rest on rocky outcrops called kopjes, which give them shade, a lookout and safe dens.» Here a lookout is a high spot where you can see far. Ask yourself which meaning fits the sentence, then try it in place of the word.
explain: Two more phrases are common. To be «on the lookout» for something is to watch and search for it. «Look out!» is a warning that means Be careful! The words beside the phrase tell you which one you are reading.
fact: A lookout is a noun, so you can have one lookout or many lookouts.
fact: «On the lookout» means watching and searching for something.
fact: «Look out!» is a warning shout that means Be careful!
fact: A lookout can be a high place, such as a tower or hilltop point, with a wide view.
fact: Informal, UK: «That's your lookout» means it is your problem.
exercise: «Meerkats take turns standing tall on their back legs as lookouts.» In this sentence, what is a lookout? | A loud noise made by hawks | A type of burrow where meerkats sleep | A tall tower made of stone | *A person or animal who keeps watch for danger
exercise: Mia shouts «Look out!» as a ball flies towards her. What does she mean? | Find the ball | *Be careful! | Come and see the view | Stop running now
exercise: Sam is «on the lookout» for his friend at the gate. What is he doing? | *Watching and searching for his friend | Asleep by the gate | Lost and cold | Playing a game with his friend
test: Walkers reached the lookout at the top of the hill and could see the whole valley. What is the lookout here? | A kind of hat | A path through the trees | *A high place with a wide view | A warning shout
test: Dad says about Tom's lost homework, «That's your lookout.» What does he mean? | *It is your problem | It is my problem | Look out for the stairs | He is sending you a gift
exam: «A rabbit thumps its back foot on the ground, because rabbits are always on the lookout.» What does «on the lookout» mean here? | Digging a new burrow | Sleeping in the sun | Hopping away from the grass | *Watching for danger
